<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anacreon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 582–c. 485 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anacreon was a Greek lyric poet from Teos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, famous for short songs about love and wine. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> conquered Ionia he joined the Teians who founded Abdera in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, and later lived at the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates of Samos</a> and of the Peisistratids in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Later imitations of his style, the Anacreontea, influenced European poetry for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anacreon.jpg" alt="Anacreon" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Gaba (User:Sting) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anacreon_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
